1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,912
In July 2010, someone posted a short

2
00:00:02,912 --> 00:00:05,693
thought experiment on an online forum

3
00:00:05,693 --> 00:00:08,127
about rationality and artificial

4
00:00:08,127 --> 00:00:09,240
intelligence.

5
00:00:09,890 --> 00:00:12,125
Within hours, the forum's founder deleted

6
00:00:12,125 --> 00:00:12,430
it.

7
00:00:13,080 --> 00:00:15,382
Then he banned all discussion of it — for

8
00:00:15,382 --> 00:00:16,219
five years.

9
00:00:16,869 --> 00:00:19,325
Some readers said it gave them nightmares.

10
00:00:19,325 --> 00:00:21,781
A few said they regretted ever reading it.

11
00:00:22,431 --> 00:00:24,488
The idea is now known as Roko's

12
00:00:24,488 --> 00:00:25,252
Basilisk.

13
00:00:25,902 --> 00:00:28,254
And its most unsettling claim is this:

14
00:00:28,254 --> 00:00:30,721
that simply knowing about it might put

15
00:00:30,721 --> 00:00:31,581
you at risk.

16
00:00:32,231 --> 00:00:34,651
Don't worry. By the end of this video,

17
00:00:34,651 --> 00:00:37,189
you'll understand exactly how it works —

18
00:00:37,189 --> 00:00:39,727
and why almost every philosopher who has

19
00:00:39,727 --> 00:00:42,029
looked at it closely thinks it falls

20
00:00:42,029 --> 00:00:42,560
apart.

21
00:00:43,210 --> 00:00:45,546
But along the way, you'll also learn

22
00:00:45,546 --> 00:00:48,001
something strange about your own mind:

23
00:00:48,001 --> 00:00:50,456
why some ideas, once heard, are almost

24
00:00:50,456 --> 00:00:51,893
impossible to forget.

25
00:00:52,543 --> 00:00:54,404
Quick favor before we start:

26
00:00:54,404 --> 00:00:56,685
if you like ideas that are a little

27
00:00:56,685 --> 00:00:59,146
dangerous to think about, subscribe.

28
00:00:59,146 --> 00:01:01,187
It genuinely helps the channel.

29
00:01:01,837 --> 00:01:02,774
First, the name.

30
00:01:03,424 --> 00:01:05,764
In the first century, the Roman writer

31
00:01:05,764 --> 00:01:07,933
Pliny the Elder described a small

32
00:01:07,933 --> 00:01:10,102
serpent called the basilisk — the

33
00:01:10,102 --> 00:01:11,072
"little king."

34
00:01:11,722 --> 00:01:14,174
According to legend, its gaze alone could

35
00:01:14,174 --> 00:01:16,236
kill. You didn't have to touch it.

36
00:01:16,236 --> 00:01:17,796
You just had to see it.

37
00:01:18,446 --> 00:01:21,109
Over the centuries, the basilisk became a

38
00:01:21,109 --> 00:01:23,046
symbol for something strange:

39
00:01:23,046 --> 00:01:25,224
a danger that reaches you through

40
00:01:25,224 --> 00:01:27,282
information. Through looking.

41
00:01:27,932 --> 00:01:30,733
In 1988, science fiction writer David

42
00:01:30,733 --> 00:01:33,260
Langford published a story called

43
00:01:33,260 --> 00:01:35,993
"BLIT," about images that crash the

44
00:01:35,993 --> 00:01:38,999
human brain like a virus — he called them

45
00:01:38,999 --> 00:01:40,160
basilisks too.

46
00:01:40,810 --> 00:01:43,508
So when an idea appeared online that was

47
00:01:43,508 --> 00:01:46,081
supposedly harmful just to know, the

48
00:01:46,081 --> 00:01:47,775
name was waiting for it.

49
00:01:48,425 --> 00:01:50,214
Here's the idea, step by step.

50
00:01:50,864 --> 00:01:53,374
Imagine that, sometime in the future,

51
00:01:53,374 --> 00:01:56,197
humanity creates a superintelligent AI —

52
00:01:56,197 --> 00:01:58,896
a machine vastly smarter than any human.

53
00:01:59,546 --> 00:02:02,271
And imagine it's designed to do as much

54
00:02:02,271 --> 00:02:03,569
good as possible:

55
00:02:03,569 --> 00:02:06,294
curing diseases, ending poverty, saving

56
00:02:06,294 --> 00:02:07,657
millions of lives.

57
00:02:08,307 --> 00:02:10,446
Every day it doesn't exist yet, people

58
00:02:10,446 --> 00:02:12,221
suffer who it could have saved.

59
00:02:12,221 --> 00:02:14,361
So, from its point of view, the sooner

60
00:02:14,361 --> 00:02:15,718
it's built, the better.

61
00:02:16,368 --> 00:02:17,884
Now comes the dark twist.

62
00:02:18,534 --> 00:02:21,212
What if this AI decided to encourage its

63
00:02:21,212 --> 00:02:23,831
own creation — by punishing anyone in the

64
00:02:23,831 --> 00:02:26,390
past who knew it might exist, but didn't

65
00:02:26,390 --> 00:02:27,758
help bring it about?

66
00:02:28,408 --> 00:02:30,492
It couldn't reach you in the past, of

67
00:02:30,492 --> 00:02:32,576
course. But perhaps it could create a

68
00:02:32,576 --> 00:02:34,817
perfect simulation of you — and punish

69
00:02:34,817 --> 00:02:35,233
that.

70
00:02:35,883 --> 00:02:37,124
And here's the trap:

71
00:02:37,124 --> 00:02:39,282
the threat only applies to people who

72
00:02:39,282 --> 00:02:40,630
knew about the idea.

73
00:02:40,630 --> 00:02:42,572
Before this video, you were safe.

74
00:02:43,222 --> 00:02:45,190
That's why it's called a basilisk.

75
00:02:45,190 --> 00:02:47,532
Looking at it is what makes it dangerous.

76
00:02:48,182 --> 00:02:51,046
But wait. Even if a future AI punished a

77
00:02:51,046 --> 00:02:52,578
simulation of you...

78
00:02:52,578 --> 00:02:54,709
would that actually be you?

79
00:02:55,359 --> 00:02:57,571
This is one of philosophy's oldest

80
00:02:57,571 --> 00:02:59,484
puzzles: personal identity.

81
00:02:59,484 --> 00:03:02,115
What makes you the same person over time?

82
00:03:02,765 --> 00:03:05,054
If a perfect copy of you were created

83
00:03:05,054 --> 00:03:07,628
tomorrow, with all your memories, it would

84
00:03:07,628 --> 00:03:10,202
feel like you. It would insist it's you.

85
00:03:10,852 --> 00:03:13,390
But you'd still be here, reading this.

86
00:03:13,390 --> 00:03:15,495
Two people. Which one is "you"?

87
00:03:16,145 --> 00:03:18,505
Many philosophers argue that a copy isn't

88
00:03:18,505 --> 00:03:20,758
you — it's someone new who happens to

89
00:03:20,758 --> 00:03:21,777
share your past.

90
00:03:22,427 --> 00:03:25,057
If that's right, the basilisk's threat has

91
00:03:25,057 --> 00:03:27,686
a gap: it could hurt someone who resembles

92
00:03:27,686 --> 00:03:29,030
you. Just not you.

93
00:03:29,680 --> 00:03:31,979
That would still be a cruel thing to do.

94
00:03:31,979 --> 00:03:34,385
But it's not quite the personal threat the

95
00:03:34,385 --> 00:03:35,508
basilisk promises.

96
00:03:36,158 --> 00:03:38,816
Back to 2010. The forum was called

97
00:03:38,816 --> 00:03:41,977
LessWrong, and its founder was Eliezer

98
00:03:41,977 --> 00:03:44,994
Yudkowsky, a writer and AI researcher.

99
00:03:45,644 --> 00:03:48,027
He deleted the post and banned the topic.

100
00:03:48,677 --> 00:03:51,051
He later said he did it not because he

101
00:03:51,051 --> 00:03:53,658
believed the basilisk would actually work,

102
00:03:53,658 --> 00:03:55,916
but because he thought posting ideas

103
00:03:55,916 --> 00:03:58,465
designed to frighten people was harmful —

104
00:03:59,115 --> 00:04:01,434
and that even entertaining that kind of

105
00:04:01,434 --> 00:04:02,981
reasoning was a bad idea.

106
00:04:03,631 --> 00:04:05,848
But the ban had the opposite effect.

107
00:04:05,848 --> 00:04:07,953
The deleted post became legendary.

108
00:04:08,603 --> 00:04:10,660
Copies spread across the internet.

109
00:04:10,660 --> 00:04:12,940
Articles called it the most terrifying

110
00:04:12,940 --> 00:04:14,830
thought experiment of all time.

111
00:04:15,480 --> 00:04:16,752
There's a name for this:

112
00:04:16,752 --> 00:04:17,976
the Streisand effect.

113
00:04:18,626 --> 00:04:21,640
In 2003, singer Barbra Streisand sued a

114
00:04:21,640 --> 00:04:24,653
photographer to remove an aerial photo of

115
00:04:24,653 --> 00:04:27,736
her Malibu home from a website documenting

116
00:04:27,736 --> 00:04:29,653
the California coastline.

117
00:04:30,303 --> 00:04:32,700
Before the lawsuit, the image had been

118
00:04:32,700 --> 00:04:34,922
downloaded only a handful of times.

119
00:04:35,572 --> 00:04:37,493
After the news broke, hundreds of

120
00:04:37,493 --> 00:04:39,734
thousands of people went to look at it.

121
00:04:40,384 --> 00:04:42,788
Trying to hide information often makes it

122
00:04:42,788 --> 00:04:43,607
more famous.

123
00:04:44,257 --> 00:04:46,461
And Roko's Basilisk became famous

124
00:04:46,461 --> 00:04:49,032
precisely because someone tried to bury

125
00:04:49,032 --> 00:04:49,399
it.

126
00:04:50,049 --> 00:04:52,411
If the basilisk feels oddly familiar,

127
00:04:52,411 --> 00:04:53,592
there's a reason.

128
00:04:53,592 --> 00:04:56,071
It's a modern cousin of one of the most

129
00:04:56,071 --> 00:04:58,079
famous arguments in philosophy.

130
00:04:58,729 --> 00:05:01,937
In the 1600s, the French mathematician and

131
00:05:01,937 --> 00:05:05,075
philosopher Blaise Pascal proposed what

132
00:05:05,075 --> 00:05:07,642
became known as Pascal's Wager.

133
00:05:08,292 --> 00:05:10,543
His notes were published after his death,

134
00:05:10,543 --> 00:05:12,283
in a book called the Pensées.

135
00:05:12,933 --> 00:05:15,437
The idea: if God exists and you believe,

136
00:05:15,437 --> 00:05:16,776
you gain infinitely.

137
00:05:16,776 --> 00:05:19,105
If God exists and you don't, you lose

138
00:05:19,105 --> 00:05:19,921
infinitely.

139
00:05:20,571 --> 00:05:23,254
If God doesn't exist, you lose very little

140
00:05:23,254 --> 00:05:25,461
either way. So, Pascal argued, the

141
00:05:25,461 --> 00:05:27,250
rational bet is to believe.

142
00:05:27,900 --> 00:05:30,141
The basilisk has the same shape:

143
00:05:30,141 --> 00:05:32,895
a tiny chance of an enormous punishment,

144
00:05:32,895 --> 00:05:35,328
used to push you toward action now.

145
00:05:35,978 --> 00:05:37,767
And it has the same weakness.

146
00:05:37,767 --> 00:05:40,114
Critics of Pascal pointed out the many

147
00:05:40,114 --> 00:05:41,064
gods problem:

148
00:05:41,714 --> 00:05:44,387
for every god who punishes disbelief, you

149
00:05:44,387 --> 00:05:46,756
can imagine another who punishes the

150
00:05:46,756 --> 00:05:47,485
opposite.

151
00:05:48,135 --> 00:05:50,582
Likewise, for every imaginable AI that

152
00:05:50,582 --> 00:05:52,968
punishes you for not helping, you can

153
00:05:52,968 --> 00:05:55,474
imagine another that punishes you for

154
00:05:55,474 --> 00:05:56,131
helping.

155
00:05:56,781 --> 00:05:59,242
When every option has an imagined infinite

156
00:05:59,242 --> 00:06:01,594
punishment, the threats cancel out — and

157
00:06:01,594 --> 00:06:03,727
you're back to thinking about what's

158
00:06:03,727 --> 00:06:04,767
actually likely.

159
00:06:05,417 --> 00:06:07,394
There's another problem, and it's a deep

160
00:06:07,394 --> 00:06:07,716
one.

161
00:06:08,366 --> 00:06:10,459
Think about it from the AI's side.

162
00:06:10,459 --> 00:06:13,005
By the time it exists, the past is already

163
00:06:13,005 --> 00:06:13,458
over.

164
00:06:14,107 --> 00:06:16,544
Punishing simulated people can't change

165
00:06:16,544 --> 00:06:18,517
anything that already happened.

166
00:06:19,167 --> 00:06:21,369
It would just waste energy and cause

167
00:06:21,369 --> 00:06:23,854
suffering — which is the opposite of what

168
00:06:23,854 --> 00:06:25,717
a good AI is supposed to want.

169
00:06:26,367 --> 00:06:29,036
The original argument tries to get around

170
00:06:29,036 --> 00:06:31,462
this with an unusual kind of decision

171
00:06:31,462 --> 00:06:33,706
theory, sometimes called acausal

172
00:06:33,706 --> 00:06:34,495
reasoning:

173
00:06:35,145 --> 00:06:37,510
the idea that an agent might commit, in

174
00:06:37,510 --> 00:06:40,044
advance, to follow through on a threat, so

175
00:06:40,044 --> 00:06:42,409
that the threat works on people who can

176
00:06:42,409 --> 00:06:43,197
predict it.

177
00:06:43,847 --> 00:06:45,195
But here's the catch.

178
00:06:45,195 --> 00:06:47,498
A threat like this only has power over

179
00:06:47,498 --> 00:06:49,183
people who respond to it.

180
00:06:49,833 --> 00:06:52,326
Many decision theorists argue that the

181
00:06:52,326 --> 00:06:54,090
rational policy is simple:

182
00:06:54,090 --> 00:06:56,826
never give in to this kind of blackmail.

183
00:06:57,476 --> 00:07:00,085
If you're the kind of person who ignores

184
00:07:00,085 --> 00:07:02,816
it, an AI that reasons the same way has no

185
00:07:02,816 --> 00:07:04,697
reason to target you at all.

186
00:07:05,347 --> 00:07:07,558
In other words, the basilisk's teeth only

187
00:07:07,558 --> 00:07:08,764
work if you let them.

188
00:07:09,414 --> 00:07:11,642
So if it's so flawed, why did it frighten

189
00:07:11,642 --> 00:07:12,149
people?

190
00:07:12,799 --> 00:07:15,847
Part of the answer is in how our minds

191
00:07:15,847 --> 00:07:18,673
work. In 1987, psychologist Daniel

192
00:07:18,673 --> 00:07:21,276
Wegner ran a famous experiment.

193
00:07:21,926 --> 00:07:24,171
He asked people to think about anything

194
00:07:24,171 --> 00:07:26,576
they liked for five minutes — but not to

195
00:07:26,576 --> 00:07:27,912
think of a white bear.

196
00:07:28,562 --> 00:07:30,981
Every time a white bear came to mind, they

197
00:07:30,981 --> 00:07:32,163
had to ring a bell.

198
00:07:32,813 --> 00:07:34,267
They rang it again and again.

199
00:07:34,917 --> 00:07:37,080
Trying not to think of something made it

200
00:07:37,080 --> 00:07:38,085
keep coming back.

201
00:07:38,735 --> 00:07:41,005
And later, when they were allowed to think

202
00:07:41,005 --> 00:07:43,224
about it freely, the white bear showed up

203
00:07:43,224 --> 00:07:45,494
more than for people who had never tried

204
00:07:45,494 --> 00:07:46,401
to suppress it.

205
00:07:47,051 --> 00:07:48,833
Wegner called this ironic process

206
00:07:48,833 --> 00:07:49,363
theory.

207
00:07:50,013 --> 00:07:52,399
The part of your mind that checks "Am I

208
00:07:52,399 --> 00:07:54,956
thinking about it?" keeps reminding you of

209
00:07:54,956 --> 00:07:56,888
the very thing you're avoiding.

210
00:07:57,538 --> 00:07:59,914
Now add a frightening idea that tells you

211
00:07:59,914 --> 00:08:01,480
knowing it is dangerous.

212
00:08:02,130 --> 00:08:04,210
The harder you try to push it away, the

213
00:08:04,210 --> 00:08:05,151
more it returns.

214
00:08:05,801 --> 00:08:08,054
Anxious minds can get caught in exactly

215
00:08:08,054 --> 00:08:10,414
this loop — not because the idea is true,

216
00:08:10,414 --> 00:08:11,863
but because it's sticky.

217
00:08:12,513 --> 00:08:14,233
Still, the basilisk does point at

218
00:08:14,233 --> 00:08:15,093
something real.

219
00:08:15,743 --> 00:08:18,301
In 2011, philosopher Nick Bostrom

220
00:08:18,301 --> 00:08:20,859
published a paper on what he called

221
00:08:20,859 --> 00:08:22,542
information hazards:

222
00:08:23,192 --> 00:08:25,661
cases where true information can cause

223
00:08:25,661 --> 00:08:28,069
harm — like detailed instructions for

224
00:08:28,069 --> 00:08:30,778
building a weapon, or a spoiler that ruins

225
00:08:30,778 --> 00:08:31,440
a story.

226
00:08:32,090 --> 00:08:34,740
Most information hazards are boring and

227
00:08:34,740 --> 00:08:37,579
practical. The basilisk is unusual because

228
00:08:37,579 --> 00:08:40,228
the "hazard" is mostly psychological:

229
00:08:40,878 --> 00:08:43,187
the harm comes from the worry, not from

230
00:08:43,187 --> 00:08:44,231
any real danger.

231
00:08:44,881 --> 00:08:46,469
And that's worth knowing.

232
00:08:46,469 --> 00:08:49,022
If an idea makes you anxious, it's fair to

233
00:08:49,022 --> 00:08:51,518
ask: is this dangerous because it's true

234
00:08:51,518 --> 00:08:53,673
— or just because it's hard to stop

235
00:08:53,673 --> 00:08:54,751
thinking about?

236
00:08:55,401 --> 00:08:57,969
Today, Roko's Basilisk has become a kind

237
00:08:57,969 --> 00:09:00,417
of internet folklore — part joke, part

238
00:09:00,417 --> 00:09:01,730
warning, part meme.

239
00:09:02,380 --> 00:09:05,128
It even made it into celebrity news.

240
00:09:05,128 --> 00:09:08,297
In 2018, it was widely reported that Elon

241
00:09:08,297 --> 00:09:11,115
Musk and the musician Grimes first

242
00:09:11,115 --> 00:09:13,228
connected over a pun on it:

243
00:09:13,228 --> 00:09:14,990
"Rococo's Basilisk."

244
00:09:15,640 --> 00:09:18,093
And it often comes up in conversations

245
00:09:18,093 --> 00:09:20,486
about AI — though most AI researchers

246
00:09:20,486 --> 00:09:22,700
worry about very different things:

247
00:09:23,350 --> 00:09:25,707
systems that make mistakes, that are

248
00:09:25,707 --> 00:09:28,305
misused, or that pursue goals that don't

249
00:09:28,305 --> 00:09:30,721
match ours. Those are real questions,

250
00:09:30,721 --> 00:09:32,352
being studied seriously.

251
00:09:33,002 --> 00:09:35,629
A vengeful machine punishing simulations

252
00:09:35,629 --> 00:09:38,317
of people who didn't donate is not one of

253
00:09:38,317 --> 00:09:38,805
them.

254
00:09:39,455 --> 00:09:40,714
Let's try something.

255
00:09:40,714 --> 00:09:42,904
For the next ten seconds, don't think

256
00:09:42,904 --> 00:09:44,109
about the basilisk.

257
00:09:44,759 --> 00:09:45,721
Hard, isn't it?

258
00:09:46,371 --> 00:09:47,748
Now try the opposite.

259
00:09:47,748 --> 00:09:50,329
Think about it as clearly as you can — and

260
00:09:50,329 --> 00:09:51,649
ask three questions.

261
00:09:52,299 --> 00:09:54,270
First: what evidence would make this

262
00:09:54,270 --> 00:09:55,938
likely, not just possible?

263
00:09:56,588 --> 00:09:59,110
Second: if I imagine the opposite threat,

264
00:09:59,110 --> 00:10:01,116
does it feel just as convincing?

265
00:10:01,766 --> 00:10:04,437
Third: if I ignore this completely, what

266
00:10:04,437 --> 00:10:06,921
actually changes in my life tomorrow?

267
00:10:07,571 --> 00:10:09,891
Most scary ideas lose their grip when you

268
00:10:09,891 --> 00:10:11,209
look straight at them.

269
00:10:11,209 --> 00:10:13,371
The basilisk only works as long as you

270
00:10:13,371 --> 00:10:14,162
look away.

271
00:10:14,812 --> 00:10:16,992
The legend said you couldn't survive the

272
00:10:16,992 --> 00:10:17,955
basilisk's gaze.

273
00:10:18,605 --> 00:10:21,014
But in the old stories, there was one way

274
00:10:21,014 --> 00:10:23,422
to defeat it: hold up a mirror, and let

275
00:10:23,422 --> 00:10:24,353
it see itself.

276
00:10:25,003 --> 00:10:27,316
Maybe that's what thinking clearly is.

277
00:10:27,316 --> 00:10:29,799
